From 6a871e154ffe4438b03b0eaf66f8fdf130d68506 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:04:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] relay: no stranger-thread close of a provider stream
 its chunk reader still reads

ManagedLlmStream pulls each provider chunk on an asyncio.to_thread worker, and
Relay pulls chunk N+1 before it hands over chunk N, so a live managed stream
almost always has a worker blocked in SSL_read. Closing the managed stream (a
/stop, a stream retry, a consumer that stops early) runs Relay's aclose on the
"relay-llm-stream-aclose" daemon; that cancelled the read and the provider
generator's finally closed the raw provider stream there, i.e. httpx closed the
socket from a stranger thread while the worker still read it. Same class as the
hub SQLite-header incidents (PR #2074, #2148): the fd number is recycled by the
next open() and the reader's SSL_read reads from / writes a TLS alert into it.
This path covers the main agent's chat/anthropic/codex streams whenever Relay
managed execution is on, not only aux calls.

Five whys:
1. Why was the TLS fd released under a reader? The provider generator's finally
   closed raw_stream on the loop thread after its to_thread read was cancelled.
2. Why was a read in flight at close? Relay prefetches the next provider chunk
   before yielding the current one, so a reader is parked on the socket between
   chunks.
3. Why did cancel not stop the reader? asyncio.to_thread cannot interrupt a
   blocking read; cancelling only detaches the awaiting coroutine.
4. Why close instead of shutdown? The relay stream path predates
   aux_provider_handoff (#2074/#2148), which established "shutdown from the
   stranger thread, close on the reading thread" for the aux siblings.
5. Why was it not caught? No test closed a managed stream while a real TLS read
   was parked; the fake streams finish their reads instantly.

Fix:
- The chunk read runs through aux_provider_handoff.run_in_thread inside a new
  collect_abandoned() block, so a read cancelled mid-flight is recorded.
- With an abandoned read, the generator's finally only shuts the stream's
  socket down (via its .response, FD-safe from any thread) and defers
  raw_stream.close() to the reading worker (release_client with a new
  shutdown= hook). No abandoned read: closed in place as before.
- ManagedLlmStream._close waits (bounded, 2 s) for abandoned reads to unwind,
  so close() returns only once the worker closed its stream and a caller's own
  later client close (close_once after a /stop) meets a quiet socket.
- ProviderAttempt gains wait(); client_scope now uses collect_abandoned.

Proof (real loopback TLS 1.3 server, tests/agent/test_relay_stream_stranger_close.py):
- RED on base: the provider stream is closed on relay-llm-stream-aclose while
  worker asyncio_0 sits in SSLSocket.read, and the fd is immediately reused
  (an eventpoll/anon-inode fd): "the reader's fd was released while it still
  read it".
- GREEN: header intact, close ran on the reader thread, tcp_shutdown=1,
  close() returns after the reader unwound; 3/3 runs with the #2074/#2148
  suites (18 passed each).
- 28 consumer files of relay_llm / aux_provider_handoff / class routing:
  756 passed, 0 failed.
